{
  "case_id": "2010-09-868",
  "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first this morning in Case 09-868, Wall v. Kholi."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Aaron L. Weisman",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The issue before the Court today is whether a State court sentence reduction motion which is a pure plea for leniency qualifies as, quote, \"an application for post-conviction or other collateral review\" within the meaning of 28 U.S.C. section 2244(d)(2)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But, Mr. Weisman, the phrase is \"post-conviction or other\" -- \"or other collateral review.\" And certainly the Rule 25 -- 35 motion is postconviction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Aaron L. Weisman",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, it can be made -- it can be made prior to when the conviction becomes final. For example, it can be made within 120 days of the imposition of sentence. Or it can be made within 120 days after the affirmance of the conviction on direct appeal of the Rhode Island Supreme Court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Would Rule 35 permit a challenge for a Federal violation? You've given an example of an illegal sentence that you think is discretionary; am I correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I don't -- I don't understand your argument at all. It seems to me the phrase \"post-conviction or other collateral review\" means postconviction collateral review or preconviction collateral review. Isn't that what is added?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. WEISMAN",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. WEISMAN: Well, what we suggest actually is very straightforward, Your Honor. If somebody captions their document, you know, \"Motion for sentence\" -- \"for sentence reduction and motion to correct an illegal sentence,\" that's not this case, obviously, because then the court -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So is the magic words \"illegal sentence\" or is the magic words \"abuse of\" -\u00ad what are the magic words?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What's to lose? You say it doesn't matter if your claim of an illegal sentence is frivolous or not. What's to lose?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. WEISMAN",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. WEISMAN: We would suggest, Your Honor, that that furthers our argument. A Rule 35 motion is not collateral review because it is not a motion -- you could say even in a legal sense, motion. It's not a motion that occurs after the judgment becomes final."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus brief",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0001-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the amicus brief in this case asserted that, in Rhode Island or in other States with a rule like this, many judges sit on these Rule 35 motions. They just let them stay pending for a considerable period of time, in order to retain some ability to modify the sentence if and when they feel like doing so."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Where?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Mizner",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MIZNER: He could correct it by filing the motion at any time, and -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What would happen if the statutory maximum for an offense in Rhode Island is 5 years and the sentencing judge imposes a sentence of 10 years, and the defense attorney at that time says, well, you can't do that, that's more than a statutory maximum; and the judge goes ahead with it, and then an appeal is taken?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Isn't -- if I look up \"collateral attack\" in Black's Law Dictionary, won't I find a definition there? And won't it tell me that this is something other than the proceeding? This is an attack on a judgment outside of the proceeding that led to the entry of that judgment. Isn't that what the term generally means?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Do you think that a petition for clemency that's presented to the governor would toll the limitations period?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Mizner",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0002-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MIZNER: I would say that the -- it becomes final when it is imposed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f56255e24aa1-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: But if the Rhode Island court has told litigants that they can't challenge some portions of an illegal sentence except by way of Rule 35, how can we call the decision on the affirmance of the conviction a final determination of the legality of the sentence? That's contradictory."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-09-868",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "f56255e24aa1d3ada1f16e94e2329e5ff669ed9cc7dd4ab3b1f9cc3c5f0c5aba",
      "evidence_id": "unavailable-f56255e24aa1-58",
      "kind": "fact",
